==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|it}} {{term|scherzo|lang=it}} "joke, play", from {{term|scherzare|lang=it}} "to joke, jest" from Old {{it}} {{term|scherzare|lang=it}}, of {{etyl|gem}} origin, from {{etyl|lng}} *{{term||skerzan|lang=lng}} "to jump merrily, enjoy oneself, jest" from {{proto|Germanic|skirtanan|to hop, jump|lang=en}} from {{proto|Indo-European|(s)kerǝd-|to dance, jump|lang=en}}. Akin to {{etyl|gmh|-}} {{term|scherzen|lang=gmh}} "to frolic, jump merrily, hop up and down" ({{de}} {{term|scherzen|lang=de}} "to joke"; {{term|Scherz|lang=de}} "joke, sport"), {{etyl|no|-}} {{term|skjerta|lang=no}} "to joke".

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/'skɛːtsəʊ/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|pl=scherzos|pl2=scherzi}}

# {{music}} A piece of music or a [[movement]] from a larger piece such as a [[symphony]]; especially, a piece of music played in a playful manner.
#* '''1980''', Anthony Burgess, ''Earthly Powers'':
#*: At seven Val knocked – three shorts and one long, out of the '''scherzo''' of Beethoven’s Fifth – and I rushed to open.

====Translations====
* Italian: {{t-|it|scherzo}}

====See also====
* [[scherzando]]

